<Project>
  <PropertyGroup>
    <!--
      Two layers of output redirection, both critical:

      1. **NuGet restore isolation** — multiple csprojs share a single
         directory in this monorepo (`src/NeoComposeUnity/` puts
         Runtime / Editor / Tests csprojs at the package root, and
         `samples/HelloWorld/` does the same). NuGet restore by
         default writes a single `obj/project.assets.json` per
         directory, and the last restore wins — dropping any
         PackageReferences declared on a previously-restored sibling
         (`NUnit` from the Tests csprojs).

      2. **Keep MSBuild output away from Unity's asset scanner** —
         the IDE shim csprojs exist purely so VSCode / Rider can
         resolve `using UnityEngine` / `using NeoCompose` over the
         sample and package source. Their `bin/Debug/.../<name>.dll`
         output, if landed inside the package directory, gets picked
         up by Unity's plugin importer and shows up as a duplicate
         test assembly alongside the real asmdef-compiled DLL —
         which would result in NUnit running both copies of every
         test. Routing both `bin/` and `obj/` to `<repo>/build/<csproj>/`
         keeps the IDE artifacts outside Unity's scan path entirely.

      The MSBuild docs require these to be set before
      `Microsoft.Common.props` imports — `Directory.Build.props` runs
      early enough.
    -->
    <BaseIntermediateOutputPath>$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)build/$(MSBuildProjectName)/obj/</BaseIntermediateOutputPath>
    <BaseOutputPath>$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)build/$(MSBuildProjectName)/bin/</BaseOutputPath>
  </PropertyGroup>
</Project>
